TITLE: CIRCLE THE SQUARE — FEATURETTE EPISODE
CREDITS: Written for Prism / ExInc
FORMAT: Screenplay (Fountain)

INT. JAN'S OFFICE - DAY

It has been a troubling few months at ExInc for CEO JAN PEACH (52, soft build, tight navy suit, comb-over). Every idea he floated appeared to fail almost immediately. He thought something must be wrong with how his stupid colleagues had implemented his suggestions. Absolutely nothing else could be the cause.

Jan would deal with that later, as for now he has a meeting with CHRISTINA DROSS (38, slim, dark bob, crisp blazer).

CHRISTINA
Morning Jan. Survived the weekend?

JAN
(sighing)
Barely. Another day dealing with these morons who cannot understand the discipline and brilliance it takes to run a place like this.

CHRISTINA
I see. Well, I might be able to help with that. I have an idea to help us communicate better with the whole department.

JAN
I'm listening.

CHRISTINA
Well, every two weeks on a Friday we do a "breakfast meeting" and offer some pastries or some other junk loaded with as much sugar as humanly possible, and we give a talk about whatever for thirty mins. We then take any questions and pretend to listen.

JAN
(enthused)
I am liking the idea of this so far. Especially if we ensure that the sugar in the breakfast junk is just enough to pacify the workers' brains so as not to care what we are talking about, but not too much so as to result in no actual work being done afterwards. This is exactly the kind of strategic thinking they don't teach you unless you've got a real MBA.

CHRISTINA
(dry)
Thought you would like it. Friday statistically is the least productive day anyway, so we could just up the sugar content as much as we like. Diminishing returns don't really apply if there were no returns to begin with.

JAN
Great. Make it so.

CHRISTINA
I am sorry, what?

JAN
You never seen Star Trek Next Generation?

CHRISTINA
(heavy sarcasm)
Shockingly no.

JAN
Fine, just make it happen. Start tomorrow with the first meeting. Now get out, I have got to prepare for a short presentation to the whole staff in a few mins.

CHRISTINA
Is this another one of your poorly rehearsed presentations?

JAN
Jesus! Everyone thinks they know better than me! I am the one with the MBA from the University of --

CHRISTINA
Made Up Place?

JAN
(screaming, red-faced)
GET OUT NOW YOU STUPID COW! JUST GET THAT DAMN MEETING ORGANISED!

Christina quickly leaves, shutting the glass door calmly behind her -- not even a flinch.

Realising he is flustered and sweating profusely, Jan unbuttons his shirt and removes it.

At that exact moment, SHARON ENFIELD (34, curvy, emerald blouse, auburn hair) enters the room without knocking.

JAN
BLOODY HELL SHARON. KNOCK ON THE DOOR WILL YOU!

Sharon gives Jan's chest a quick glance. His heavy manscaping shows his chest and stomach hair shaved into a large arrow pointing directly towards his groin.

SHARON
(unbothered)
I hear Christina is doing breakfast meetings now.

JAN
I really don't have time for this now Sharon. I can't make any concessions for you to have some other nonsensical meeting --

SHARON
Well I have needs too Jan that must be met.

Jan doesn't need to be told again. He lowers the blinds and locks his office door.


INT. CORRIDOR / OPEN-PLAN FLOOR - CONTINUOUS

CHRIS (32, lean, light blue shirt) and RICK (40, broad, grey polo) stand by a desk run.

CHRIS
Christ! They don't even try to hide it any more do they.

RICK
(flat)
Never have. Give it another five minutes.

They watch Sharon leave Jan's room dripping in sweat, with dishevelled hair and makeup.

Minutes later, Jan emerges (shirt re-buttoned askew) and claps his hands to gather everyone around.

JAN
Right guys, as you know --

CHRIS
Does Sharon get a pass on attending this?

JAN
Err... yes she does. I have given her the rest of the day off for personal reasons.

Quiet sniggering ripples through the assembled crowd of worker drones.

JAN
(flushed)
SHUT UP! I am truly appalled by the lack of discipline in this place and that changes now! I have decided a new project is required to manage all the change around here.

RICK
What happened to the last project for this, isn't it ongoing? By that I mean completely failing.

JAN
There's no need for the previous project as everything has been a great success even though most things were not delivered on time or within budget. I know because I see everything happening so am best placed to judge. We need a new project to continue the success of the previous project. So I have decided to call the project Inception.

CHRIS
(shouting to laughter)
You're dreaming Jan!

JAN
What?!

CHRIS
Inception is the name of a film about dreams Jan.

JAN
(flustered)
Oh, well it is also the name of this project.

Jan realizes it is too late to change the name, as he has already ordered 1,000 stress balls, pens, and t-shirts with "PROJECT INCEPTION" printed on them.

CHRIS
Will there be a lead for this?

JAN
At last something sensible is asked. Yes there will. However, it is with regret that I have to inform you all that the position has already been filled... by me.

GROANS echo from the crowd.

JAN
Yes groan all you like, but I am the one with the most talent and skills to deliver this. It will add £50k to my salary as I simply add this role into my duties. I will let you know when more information is available, now GET BACK TO WORK!


INT. STAFF RESTAURANT / CANTEEN - NEXT MORNING

Lots of pain au chocolat have been prepared by the canteen team to a specific high-sugar recipe given by Christina. Even the canteen staff thought the sugar content was far too high, but Christina assured them it was fine.

Due to the free food, there is high demand. The last pastry has just been claimed before Jan enters.

JAN
Is there any more pain au chocolat?

CANTEEN WORKER
Sorry, all gone.

JAN
(veins bulging)
OH THAT IS IT!

Jan grabs the plates from the counter and sweeps them onto the floor.

CRASH! China shatters. Everyone in the canteen stops talking and stares.

JAN
(screaming)
I HAVE HAD IT WITH THIS PLACE! I HAVE AN MBA, NOBODY APPRECIATES MY IMMENSE TALENT!

Jan grabs a heavy meeting chair and hurls it into the nearest window.

GLASS SHATTERS.

Jan turns to grab a second chair. Suddenly --

POP-CRACKLE!

A loud electric discharge sound behind Jan. Jan freezes, drops the chair, and slumps face-first onto the floor unconscious.

Behind him stands RICK, holding a prop TASER.

CHRIS
(crouching down)
Have you killed him?

RICK
(calmly stowing Taser)
No relax, he will be out for a while. I knew this Taser would come in useful one day in this place. I think we need the police here...

FADE OUT.


= SCENE 4: THE NEXT DAY

FADE IN:

INT. JAN'S OFFICE - THE NEXT DAY

Jan sits behind his desk in a foam neck brace, still wearing yesterday's shirt, buttoned wrong. A butterfly plaster on his forehead is, unmistakably, shaped like a chair leg. On the desk sits a small mountain of PROJECT INCEPTION stress balls. He is compulsively squeezing one, rhythmically, like a stress-ball metronome.

Christina enters with a tablet and a coffee she has no intention of sharing.

CHRISTINA
You're alive then. Shame.

JAN
(wounded)
It takes more than eight thousand volts to put Jan Peach down, Christina. I've decided to reframe it internally as a live team-cohesion stress-test. Very on-brand for Inception, if you think about it.

CHRISTINA
Rick tasered you in front of forty witnesses, the canteen's insurance assessor, and a man from Environmental Health who was only there about the extractor fan.

JAN
Yes, well. Nolan films are famously misunderstood on first viewing.

CHRISTINA
(dry)
It wasn't a film, Jan. You threw a chair through a window and collapsed face-first into a tray of pain au chocolat crumbs.

JAN
(pause, genuinely proud)
Did I get a good crumb-to-face ratio? I don't remember that part.

CHRISTINA
Ninety percent coverage. HR has photos. For the file.

JAN
(sudden panic)
There's a FILE?!

CHRISTINA
There's several. Legal opened one. So did the landlord. So did, somehow, a man on LinkedIn calling himself a "workplace culture strategist" who is already offering us a keynote.

JAN
(brightening immediately)
Book him.

CHRISTINA
Jan.

JAN
BOOK. HIM. If a man on the internet believes I have "culture," I am not going to be the one to argue.

Christina exhales slowly through her nose, the exhale of a woman recalculating her entire career.

CHRISTINA
There's also the small matter of you being tasered by your own Head of Facilities.

JAN
Rick was well within his rights. I was clearly out of control. I could've seriously injured myself on that second chair, and then where would this company be? Chairless AND Jan-less. It's called risk management. I invented it. Well -- not invented. Studied. At my MBA.

CHRISTINA
(flat)
The University of Made Up Place.

JAN
(through gritted teeth)
Buckinghamshire College of Advanced Enterprise Studies.

CHRISTINA
Which closed in 2011.

JAN
Which closed WELL. With dignity. Unlike this canteen window.

CHRISTINA
Speaking of which -- HR needs you to complete an Incident Reflection Form before you're cleared to re-enter any room with furniture in it.

JAN
(sighing, extending a hand)
Fine. Give it here.

CHRISTINA
It's fourteen pages.

JAN
FOURTEEN?! For a taser?!

CHRISTINA
Page one is the taser. Pages two through fourteen are follow-up questions about your "relationship with authority," prompted by page one.

JAN
(muttering, taking it)
This is worse than the coversheet memo.

CHRISTINA
(already leaving)
Everything is worse than the coversheet memo, Jan. That's why we keep sending it.


INT. OPEN-PLAN FLOOR - CONTINUOUS

CHRIS and RICK stand near the same desk run as before. Behind them, a maintenance man is taping cardboard over the canteen's broken window, visible through the glass partition.

CHRIS
So. You're a hero now.

RICK
(flat)
I'm a man who keeps a taser in his desk. The company decided which noun to use.

CHRIS
HR called it "a proportionate use of de-escalation equipment." I called it "the best thing I've seen since the Christmas party."

RICK
There's more where that came from.

CHRIS
More tasers?

RICK
(opening a desk drawer just enough for Chris to see in)
Bear spray. Zip ties. A second taser, in case the first one needs a rest. A flare gun I'm not licensed for.

CHRIS
Why do you have a flare gun in an office building?

RICK
(closing the drawer)
Because one day someone's going to ask me that question in a much worse tone of voice, and I intend to be ready.

Nina from Payroll passes by carrying a stack of forms.

NINA
Incident Reflection Forms! Everyone needs to sign and return an Incident Reflection Form! Incident Reflection Forms!

(repeats this, receding down the row, exactly like her phone greeting)

CHRIS
(taking one, not reading it)
I'm not filling this in.

RICK
You have to.

CHRIS
I watched a man get electrocuted over pastries, Rick. I don't need fourteen pages to reflect on it. My reflection is: correct outcome, wrong voltage, should've been higher.

He drops the form, unsigned, into the recycling bin on his way past. A DELIVERY MAN wheels in a trolley stacked with boxes marked PROJECT INCEPTION 2: THE RECKONING -- MERCHANDISE (PHASE TWO).

RICK
(reading the boxes, dead calm)
He ordered more.

CHRIS
He ordered more.

RICK
Before or after the taser?

CHRIS
Tracking number says the order went through while he was unconscious on the canteen floor.

RICK
(a long beat)
Christina must have his card details memorised at this point.

CHRIS
Christina IS his card details at this point.

Sharon walks past, entirely unbothered, carrying her own Incident Reflection Form already completed in full, in green gel pen, with a small drawn heart dotting the "i" in "Incident."

SHARON
(cheerfully, to no one)
Mine was easy. I wasn't even in the room.

CHRIS
(watching her go)
No. No, you were absolutely in a room.

RICK
Different room. Timing's the only thing keeping HR out of it.

CHRIS
Barely.


INT. HR BREAKOUT ROOM - LATER

A laminated sign on the door reads MANDATORY: RESPECT AT WORK REFRESHER (30 MINS). Inside, folding chairs face a wheeled TV/DVD combo unit that looks older than most of the staff. DEBBIE VANCE (45, HR lanyard, relentless, unkillable positivity) stands beside it with a remote.

Jan and Sharon sit in the front row, side by side, disturbingly close for a "Respect at Work" session, sharing a bag of crisps.

DEBBIE
Right! Thank you all for making time for this. As you know, following recent events --

JAN
(loudly, to the room)
Unrelated events.

DEBBIE
-- following recent, unrelated events, Head Office has asked every department to complete the refresher. It's just a formality. Fifteen questions, multiple choice, and then we can all get back to our desks feeling very good about our culture.

She presses play. A VERY OLD TRAINING VIDEO plays, tinny audio, VHS tracking lines.

VIDEO NARRATOR (V.O.)
(tinny)
Welcome to "Boundaries: Working Better Together." Let's meet Gary, who has made an inappropriate comment to a colleague...

JAN
(leaning over to Sharon, stage-whisper)
This Gary sounds like an idiot.

SHARON
(not whispering at all)
Gary sounds like every man I've ever managed.

JAN
(delighted)
HA! Managed. Good one.

He high-fives her. Debbie does not react, having long since stopped reacting to anything that happens in this room.

DEBBIE
(reading off a clipboard, deadpan)
Question one. "Is it appropriate to lock your office door during working hours with a colleague inside?"

JAN
(hand shooting up)
Depends entirely on the fire code.

DEBBIE
(not looking up)
It's multiple choice, Jan.

JAN
Then it's C. Always C. C is "depends on context," I've read ahead.

DEBBIE
There is no C. There's A, "yes," and B, "no."

JAN
(long pause)
...B?

DEBBIE
(already stamping his form)
Perfect. Moving on.

Sharon leans back in her chair, completely serene, eating another crisp.

SHARON
This is the most action this room's seen since the fire drill.

DEBBIE
(monotone, not looking up)
The fire drill was also about you, Sharon.

SHARON
(brightly)
Was it?

DEBBIE
You set off the alarm on your way out of Jan's office. Twice. In one week. We changed the sensor sensitivity because of you specifically. There's a memo. It's called the Enfield Protocol.

Jan beams with something that might be pride.

JAN
(to Sharon, warmly)
They named a protocol after you. That's more than they ever gave me.

SHARON
They gave you a form, love.

JAN
Fourteen pages of form.


INT. STAFF CANTEEN - THAT AFTERNOON

The broken window is now boarded with plywood and a hand-written sign: DO NOT LEAN ON WINDOW (NEW). Staff eat lunch warily, giving the boarded section a wide berth. MAUREEN, the canteen worker from yesterday, is back behind the counter, restocking a tray of pain au chocolat with visible reluctance.

Jan enters, neck brace now swapped for a smaller, "more dignified" one, trailed by Christina wheeling a trolley of PROJECT INCEPTION 2 merchandise -- tote bags, mugs, a banner reading INCEPTION 2: DEEPER THIS TIME.

JAN
(clapping hands, addressing the room)
Right, everyone! In light of yesterday's, ah, learning opportunity, I've decided the best way forward is not to retreat, but to double down. So! I am relaunching the initiative under a bold new name.

CHRIS
(from a table, not looking up)
Please don't say Inception 2.

JAN
Inception 2: The Reckoning.

Groans, louder than the ones for the original announcement. MAUREEN, behind the counter, stops restocking and just watches, tongs frozen mid-air.

CHRISTINA
(quietly, to Jan, through her teeth)
We talked about this. The lawyers specifically said do not use the word "reckoning" near an incident involving a weapon.

JAN
(waving her off, to the room)
Everyone gets a tote bag! Everyone gets a mug! And to really bring the team together, we are doing something Christina came up with -- a "trust fall" exercise, right here, right now, in this very canteen.

MAUREEN
(from behind the counter, flat)
Not near the pastries.

JAN
Especially near the pastries! That's where the ENERGY is, Maureen!

He grabs a stress ball from a tote bag and hurls it, overhand, at Chris in a misguided attempt at "team energy." It sails past Chris and knocks a full tray of pain au chocolat clean out of Maureen's hands.

CRASH. The tray hits the floor.

A beat of total silence. Everyone looks at the wreckage, then at Jan, then, very deliberately, at Rick, who has just walked in for his lunch and is now standing in the doorway holding a tray, taking in the scene with the weary calm of a man who has done this exact math before.

RICK
(to Chris, not even raising his voice)
Same time as yesterday?

CHRIS
Give it about ninety seconds.

Jan, oblivious, has already picked up a second stress ball.

JAN
(loudly, arms wide)
WHO WANTS TO CATCH THE NEXT ONE?!

Christina slowly lowers her face into her hands. Sharon, at a nearby table, files a nail and doesn't look up. Rick sets down his tray, unhurried, and reaches -- not for the taser this time, but for his phone, thumbing it open to a local news app already bookmarked.

RICK
(reading off the screen, deadpan, to the room)
Well. Good news is the local paper's run the story already. Bad news is the headline's "EXINC CEO 'STRESS BALLS' STAFF AFTER TASER INCIDENT."

JAN
(freezing mid-throw, delighted)
We're in the PAPER?!

CHRISTINA
(muffled, hands still over her face)
That is not the takeaway, Jan.

JAN
(beaming, to the whole canteen)
Get the marketing team on the phone. We need more stress balls.

FADE OUT.
